00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.940
Since November 2, 2000, the International
Space Station has been continuously crewed

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.380
meaning humanity has had a permanent presence
in space.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.140
This importance of this station cannot be
overstated—NASA has literally filled a book

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.450
with all the ways the ISS has benefited humans
on earth.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:31.279
It’s helped develop new cancer treatments,
enhanced robotic surgery technology, improved

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:35.329
data processing techniques, its benefits stretch
far and wide.

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:40.609
It is quite possibly the most significant
science experiment humanity has ever undertaken.

00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:46.000
It is also quite possibly the most expensive
single item humanity has ever built at an

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.079
overall cost, so far, of about $160 billion.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:58.879
Breaking that down, each day that each astronaut
spends on the ISS costs about $7.5 million.

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:03.649
The ISS is a joint project between Russia’s
Roscosmos agency, Japan’s JAXA, Europe’s

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:06.610
ESA, Canada’s CSA, and the US’ NASA.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.640
Physically, though, it’s split up into the
Russian segment and the US segment with these

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.920
two countries more or less taking responsibility
for managing and supplying their side.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.980
At over 350 feet or 110 meters long, the station
is longer than a 747 and has more interior

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.630
space than the average six bedroom house.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.329
Space is still at a premium, though, as science
experiments and equipment take up most of

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:33.180
the room so normally there are only a maximum
of six crew members onboard at any given time.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.329
Back on earth, though, there are thousands
of people working to support the activities

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:38.840
of those six.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.340
Now that the international space station is
built and operating, the whole mission is

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.599
basically a big logistics problem—it involves
getting people, food, water, supplies, and

00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:52.539
experiments up and down from the station in
a consistent, cost-effective, and safe manner.

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:58.460
Food, as an example, is one of the only completely
non-renewable resources onboard the ISS.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.929
Water can be recycled, oxygen can be generated,
supplies can be reused, but food is one and

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:04.929
done.

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:08.890
That means there has to be consistent shipments
from earth to the station.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.030
About half the food is supplied by the Russians
and the other half by the Americans.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.690
There are a few constraints to space food—it
has to be shelf stable as there are no refrigerators

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:21.830
onboard, it has to last about two years as
resupplies only happen a few times a year,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.750
it has to be light as weight is precious on
the resupply rockets, and it has to be easy

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.390
to prepare as astronauts’ time is valuable.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.140
Within those parameters, the Russians use
a system of canned food while the Americans

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.810
use a system of flexible pouches which have
the advantage of being lighter weight than

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.860
the cans.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.390
This NASA food is produced at the Johnson
Space Center in Houston, Texas.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.950
Long before they launch, the astronauts do
a taste test to determine which foods they

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.680
like most and they express these preferences.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.170
Based off those, NASA will produce and load
more of the best liked foods.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.920
The astronauts can choose from hundreds of
items—spaghetti and meatballs, barbecue

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.450
beef brisket, Caribbean chicken, vegetable
quiche, beef enchiladas, the options go on

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.450
and on.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.290
Somewhat surprisingly, the ingredients for
the food are just purchased at a grocery store

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.730
like any other meal.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:11.190
There’s no special sourcing or special ingredients,
although they do inspect the ingredients carefully

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.120
after buying to be extra sure that they’re
safe.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.370
As far as we know, no astronaut has yet gotten
food poisoning in space and NASA wants to

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.540
keep it that way.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.890
The food is then prepared fairly conventionally
until the last step.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.850
Food is made shelf stable and lightweight
by one of two methods—thermostabilization

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.070
or freeze drying.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.400
With thermostabilization, heat is used to
destroy microorganisms and enzymes that would

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.030
cause food to spoil.With freeze drying, the
food is frozen and then almost all moisture

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.030
is removed.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.000
With this technique, there is nearly no water
left in the food, which is normally the bulk

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.780
of its weight, so a full serving of spaghetti,
for example, weighs only one ounce or 28 grams.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.360
The space station has a water recycling system
that recovers about 80% of the water onboard

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.910
which means that, while they do occasionally
have to resupply water from earth, it’s

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.820
more efficient to ship food up without water
in it since the water onboard can be reused

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.010
for multiple meals and that also helps keep
it shelf stable.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:08.240
Overall, freeze drying reduces the total food
weight they have to take up.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.500
NASA currently pays about $29,000 per pound
for shipment to the Space Station.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.850
Each gallon of water they bring therefore
costs them about a quarter of a million dollars.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:24.180
That also means each of those one ounce portions
of spaghetti costs them about $1,800.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.930
They’ll also typically include a few pieces
of fresh fruit anytime a rocket goes up as

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:35.030
a treat and, with these shipment costs, each
apple is worth almost $10,000.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.360
This is why so much focus is put on minimizing
weight.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:42.600
In addition to the food they make, some foods
are just shipped up as is.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.370
For example, if a crew member has a particular
type of granola bar they like, the food preparation

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.350
team will go out and buy some of those as
a sample and perform some tests in their lab

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.640
to figure out if it’s, as they call it,
“flight compatible.”

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.180
In that, they’re basically looking to be
sure that it’s not too crumbly as they don’t

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.810
want crumbs floating around into all the nooks
and crannies of the station, as well as being

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.530
sure it’s not too liquidy, both for the
purposes of weight and to be sure that it

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.810
doesn’t cause free liquid to float around.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:12.210
Assuming it passes these tests, astronauts
can pretty much bring what they want for snacks.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.660
It will be repackaged into NASA’s pouches,
but there’s a surprising amount of simply

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.220
store bought food on the station.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:23.620
Once all food is produced and packaged, it
is shipped to one of four launch sites—either

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:28.200
the Mid-Atlantic Regional Spaceport in Virginia
where Northrop Grumman Cygnus resupply spacecrafts

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.810
are launched from; Cape Canaveral, Florida
where SpaceX Dragon spacecraft are launched

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.310
from; Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakstan where
Russian Progress resupply and Soyuz crewed

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:41.150
spacecraft are launched from; or Tanegashima
Space Center in Japan where Japanese Kounotori

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.330
spacecraft are launched from.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.190
The food will be carefully packed alongside
everything else making the trip ensuring proper

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.680
spacecraft balance.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.340
Thanks to all these weight-saving techniques,
supplies for crew, including food, only represent

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.420
a small portion of the overall weight of each
launch.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:01.160
For example, on one SpaceX launch, crew supplies
only represented 530 pounds or 240 kilograms

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.690
of the 6,000 pounds or 2,700 kilograms of
cargo.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:08.660
The rest of the launch capacity is dedicated
to equipment and experiments.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:14.460
Now, part of the difficulty of this supply
chain is that launch dates change a lot.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:20.210
For example, the last SpaceX resupply, CRS-16,
was initially scheduled for August 2018, then

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.160
it was pushed back till the 29 of November,
then till December 4th, then again to December

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.860
5th when it finally launched.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.600
These delays can come from quite mundane issues.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.310
The December 4th to December 5th delay, for
example, came because it was found that the

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:37.950
food loaded for lab mice who were making the
trip was moldy and had to be replaced.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.800
Even just getting a launch or landing date
in the first place is difficult enough.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.300
Each site has its own set of constraints potentially
preventing use.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.530
For example, Tanegashima Space Center in Japan
can only be used for launch at certain times

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:53.449
of year since, during fishing season, there
are large numbers of ships offshore from the

00:06:53.449 --> 00:06:57.040
launch site which could be hit and damaged
if a rocket exploded.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.419
Elsewhere, a Soyuz landing in Kazakstan had
to be rescheduled as there was a G8 summit

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:05.840
nearby which, for security reasons, imposed
airspace restrictions that prevented spacecraft

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.050
search and rescue aircraft from flying.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.970
Even the angle of the sun relative to the
ISS constrains when a spacecraft can dock.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:17.400
The other factor that can affect the supply
chain is that rockets are imperfect.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.330
5 of the 117 resupply missions to date to
the International Space Station did not make

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:26.400
it for one reason or another meaning there’s
a failure rate of 4.3%.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.630
There was even a nine month stretch from 2014
to 2015 when three resupply missions failed.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:37.620
In times like these, it’s quite important
that the ISS has plenty of backup food, water,

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:38.710
and supplies.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:42.230
While the astronauts would never starve, running
out of food or water could mean that they

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:46.610
would have to return to earth early potentially
leaving the ISS un-crewed and therefore wasting

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.410
many millions or billions of dollars.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:54.030
When that third mission failed on June 28th,
2015, the station had enough food to last

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.400
them for about another fifty days.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.010
In this case, even with three failed missions,
they had plenty of food to last them especially

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:05.060
considering the next resupply mission, a Russian
Progress spacecraft, was scheduled just a

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.030
week after the failure and it was successful.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.870
There have been close calls, though.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.880
After the Space Shuttle Columbia disaster
in 2003, astronauts had to cut their daily

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.210
caloric intake and supplies got as low as
7 to 14 days of food.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.960
They were one resupply failure away from having
to evacuate.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:26.070
Sometimes, in order to stretch the food supply,
NASA will have to extend the certified shelf

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.810
life of food onboard.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.990
For this purpose, they keep a sample of each
batch of food they make on earth, packaged

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:36.430
and stored in the exact same conditions as
on the ISS, so they can test if food is still

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.360
fit for consumption and safe.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.740
Assuming a rocket doesn’t have any issues,
it will typically make it to the ISS in about

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:44.459
two to three days.

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:48.699
Once the spacecraft is near the ISS, the crew
will spend almost a whole working day dealing

00:08:48.699 --> 00:08:50.350
with the docking process.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:55.279
Once that’s completed, either the same day
or the next morning, they’ll open the hatch.

00:08:55.279 --> 00:08:58.139
Typically right at the hatch is the crew care
package.

00:08:58.139 --> 00:09:02.450
This will include those fresh fruit and vegetables
as well as some items sent up by the crew’s

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.450
families.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.339
One time, the crew care package even included
real ice cream.

00:09:06.339 --> 00:09:10.670
While there isn’t normally a freezer onboard,
in this instance a portable freezer was sent

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:15.550
up in order to transport samples from an experiment
back to earth but, since it was empty on the

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:17.600
trip up, the crew got this treat.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.649
Over the next few months, astronauts will
go through their food one container at a time

00:09:21.649 --> 00:09:25.589
scanning a barcode each time they open one
to let mission control know what their inventory

00:09:25.589 --> 00:09:27.069
level is like.

00:09:27.069 --> 00:09:30.970
That way, if the food is going quicker or
more slowly than expected NASA can adjust

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.300
future shipments.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:36.720
Onboard, dehydrated food is heated up and
hydrated using a food re-hydrator while other

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.139
food can be heated up using another heater.

00:09:39.139 --> 00:09:43.649
The point of putting so much effort into food
on the ISS is for the psychological well-being

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:45.010
of the astronauts.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:49.309
With working long hours bottled up in a small
space for up to a year, the mental stress

00:09:49.309 --> 00:09:54.670
of the job is enormous so any effort that
can help reduce that is effort worth doing.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.550
As part of this, NASA puts a good amount of
work into movie night.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.850
They have a large projector screen and, in
addition to their onboard digital library

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:06.560
of about 500 movies, the crew can download
new movies.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.519
The ISS does have an internet connection and
they’ve been known to get some movies, such

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:13.990
as Star Wars: The Last Jedi, while they’re
still in theaters.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:17.879
That same internet connection lets astronauts
browse the web quite freely, even if it’s

00:10:17.879 --> 00:10:18.879
not quick.

00:10:18.879 --> 00:10:22.970
While the station does have 300 megabits per
second of downlink capacity, the vast majority

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:27.129
of it is dedicated to experiment and other
data, but astronauts are able to regularly

00:10:27.129 --> 00:10:29.470
video call their families using it.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:34.480
The unloading process of resupply vessels
is quite a slow process, partially just because

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.970
there isn’t much storage space onboard.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:41.199
Once a resupply vessel is unloaded, though,
the crew will start loading it again with

00:10:41.199 --> 00:10:42.199
trash.

00:10:42.199 --> 00:10:46.490
The Northrop Grumman Cygnus, Russian Progress,
and Japanese Kounotori spacecraft cannot return

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.510
to earth so they just burn up in the atmosphere
along with all the trash.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:54.819
The SpaceX Dragon and crewed Russian Soyuz
spacecrafts do return to earth so they can

00:10:54.819 --> 00:10:57.079
bring back samples and experiments.

00:10:57.079 --> 00:11:01.740
Once ready, the spacecraft will undock and
slowly drift away from the ISS until it disappears

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:07.220
from view and at that point, the ISS is ready
for another delivery of humans or cargo—one

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:11.819
of the most unique regular deliveries in our
universe.

00:11:11.819 --> 00:11:15.089
Rocket science is something that always seems
out of reach for mere mortals.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:19.339
It’s something that’s supposed to be the
most confusing and complex area of study but

